<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools"
    package="com.adsf.revenue">

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

    <!--
      No storage permissions, and none are coming.

      The workbook export writes into the app's own external directory
      (lib/export/workbook_saver_io.dart), which has needed no permission since
      Android 10. The statement importer that is still to be written will go
      through the system file picker, which hands over the one file the person
      chose and needs no permission either. So neither the feature that exists
      nor the feature that is planned wants READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE or
      WRITE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE — they would only add a "Files and media" line to
      the Play listing and a question to the Data safety form.

      What was here before: READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE, WRITE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE,
      android:requestLegacyExternalStorage (ignored since API 30), and
      READ_INTERNAL_STORAGE — which is not an Android permission at all. There
      has never been a permission for an app's own internal storage, because
      an app has never needed one to read it. Nothing in lib/ ever requested
      any of the four.
    -->

    <!--
      Declared because the menu importer drives the camera itself rather than
      handing the job to another app — see lib/settings/menu_capture_page.dart
      for why it has to. A borrowed camera needs no permission here; an opened
      sensor does, and Android asks the person for it at first use.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.CAMERA" />

    <!--
      camera_android_camerax brings both of these in through manifest merging,
      and both are wrong for this app.

      RECORD_AUDIO is there because CameraX can record video with sound. This
      app takes still photographs of a menu and has no audio path at all, but
      the merged manifest is what Play reads, so without this removal the
      listing advertises microphone access that nothing uses — and the Data
      safety form has to account for it.

      WRITE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE (maxSdkVersion 28) is the same story from the
      other end: nothing writes to shared storage, on any API level.

      READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE has to be named too, and it is the one that is easy
      to miss. Nobody declares it — the manifest merger *invents* it. Removing
      the WRITE permission above is not enough, because the merger adds the
      READ permission on its own as soon as any library in the graph asks to
      write, on the reasoning that an app which can write shared storage
      presumably reads it. The build log says so in as many words:

        uses-permission#android.permission.READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE
        IMPLIED ... reason: io.flutter.plugins.camerax requested
        WRITE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE

      Verified against the merged manifest rather than reasoned about: the
      first attempt removed RECORD_AUDIO and WRITE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE, and
      READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE was still in the release build afterwards.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECORD_AUDIO"
        tools:node="remove" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.WRITE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE"
        tools:node="remove" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE"
        tools:node="remove" />

    <!-- The camera is a convenience, not a requirement: Choose image works on
         a device without one, so this must not narrow who can install. -->
    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.camera" android:required="false" />

    <!--
      And this is the one that actually decided it, which the line above did
      not. camera_android_camerax merges in `android.hardware.camera.any` with
      no `android:required`, and an omitted `required` means **true** — so the
      merged manifest asked Play to hide the app from every device without a
      camera, which is the opposite of what the line above intends. Two
      different feature names: declaring `android.hardware.camera` optional
      says nothing about `android.hardware.camera.any`.

      tools:replace rather than a second plain declaration, because the merger
      keeps the stricter value on a conflict unless told which one wins.
    -->
    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.camera.any"
        android:required="false"
        tools:replace="android:required" />

    <!--
      Package visibility, for url_launcher only.

      Since Android 11 an app cannot see what else is installed unless it says
      what it is looking for. url_launcher resolves a link by asking
      `intent.resolveActivity(packageManager)` first, and under that filtering
      the call returns null for a browser it is not allowed to see — so
      `Source code` (Store → Account & app) and the Play-listing fallback in
      lib/settings/app_update.dart both failed silently on every Android 11
      and later device. Not "no browser installed": no permission to notice
      one.

      This is deliberately narrow. It asks to see apps that open https links
      and the Play Store, and nothing else.

      It is also why the note below about ACTION_IMAGE_CAPTURE is still true
      and not a contradiction: there the missing app is genuinely missing, and
      being allowed to look for it would not conjure one. Here the browser
      exists and only the looking was blocked.
    -->
    <queries>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
            <data android:scheme="https" />
        </intent>
        <package android:name="com.android.vending" />
    </queries>

    <!--
      No <queries> for ACTION_IMAGE_CAPTURE, deliberately. Package visibility
      would only let this app *see* a camera app; it cannot conjure one, and on
      a device whose camera app was replaced or disabled there is nothing to
      see. The importer opens the sensor itself instead, so what other apps are
      installed stops mattering — lib/settings/menu_capture_page.dart.
    -->

   <application
        android:label="Revenue"
        android:name="${applicationName}"
        android:icon="@mipmap/launcher_icon"
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:fullBackupContent="false"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
        >
        <!--
          Auto Backup off, because of the offline queue.

          The only thing this app keeps on the device is SharedPreferences: the
          theme choice, and `pendingOrders.v1` — orders rung up with no
          connection that have not reached Firestore yet
          (lib/database/pending_order_queue.dart). Backing that up means Google
          restores a queue of unsent orders onto a *different* device, which
          then sends them: the same takings booked twice, in a shop's books,
          with nothing on screen to explain it. A theme preference is not worth
          that risk, and it costs a tap to set again.

          android:extractNativeLibs is also gone from here. It was "true",
          which forces the legacy packaging AGP 8 otherwise avoids: the .so
          files get copied out of the APK at install time instead of being
          mapped from it, so the install is roughly the size of the libraries
          again for no benefit.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:launchMode="singleTop"
            android:theme="@style/LaunchTheme"
            android:configChanges="orientation|keyboardHidden|keyboard|screenSize|smallestScreenSize|locale|layoutDirection|fontScale|screenLayout|density|uiMode"
            android:hardwareAccelerated="true"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize">
            <!-- Specifies an Android theme to apply to this Activity as soon as
                 the Android process has started. This theme is visible to the user
                 while the Flutter UI initializes. After that, this theme continues
                 to determine the Window background behind the Flutter UI. -->
            <meta-data
              android:name="io.flutter.embedding.android.NormalTheme"
              android:resource="@style/NormalTheme"
              />
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>
        <!-- Don't delete the meta-data below.
             This is used by the Flutter tool to generate GeneratedPluginRegistrant.java -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="flutterEmbedding"
            android:value="2" />
    </application>
</manifest>
